Zoho CRM and HubSpot are two of the best-known CRMs for small teams, and they price themselves very differently. Zoho sells four paid editions with flat per-user prices. HubSpot sells its CRM in three editions and separately sells Sales Hub seats, with promotions that change what a new customer pays. This guide sets out what each vendor publishes, works out a yearly cost for a five-person team, and notes where the numbers are unclear. It does not rank the two on ease of use or feature quality, because we have not run them side by side. A disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the vendor pages cited below, not by using the products ourselves. At the time of writing, Stackbrook has no affiliate relationship with Zoho or HubSpot. For the free tiers of each, see our separate guides to [HubSpot's free CRM](/posts/hubspot-free-crm-limits) and [Zoho CRM's free edition](/posts/zoho-crm-free-edition).

## What each vendor lists

Zoho's pricing pages list four paid editions in US dollars. Billed annually they are Standard at $14, Professional at $23, Enterprise at $40 and Ultimate at $52 per user per month. Zoho's 2022 edition PDF lists monthly-billing prices of $20, $35, $50 and $65, though only the Enterprise page's FAQ states a monthly figure in text ($50 against $40 annual), so treat the other monthly figures as dated. Zoho's India pages list the same editions in rupees, so prices differ by country.

HubSpot's Smart CRM page lists Starter, Professional and Enterprise. Starter lists at $20 per seat per month, and HubSpot's Starter page currently advertises new customers $7 a month per seat on an annual commitment or $10 month to month, for a limited time. HubSpot's own blog gives Professional as $45 per seat per month on an annual commitment or $50 month to month, and Enterprise as $75. HubSpot's Smart CRM page itself lists Professional only as starting at $50 a month per seat, so the $45 annual figure comes from HubSpot's blog and may not match what you see at checkout.

| Zoho CRM | Per user per month, billed annually | HubSpot Smart CRM | Per seat per month |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Free | $0 for up to 3 users | Free | $0 for 2 users |
| Standard | $14 | Starter | $20 list ($7 annual promotion for new customers) |
| Professional | $23 | Professional | $50 listed ($45 annual per HubSpot's blog) |
| Enterprise | $40 | Enterprise | $75 (billing basis not stated) |
| Ultimate | $52 | | |

![Dot plot of yearly list cost for five CRM users, from Zoho CRM Standard at 840 dollars to HubSpot Smart CRM Professional at 2,700 dollars](/images/site1/zoho-vs-hubspot-crm-cost-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from Zoho's and HubSpot's published pricing pages.*

## A five-person team, per year

Multiplying list prices by five users and twelve months gives these figures, our own arithmetic with annual billing unless noted.

| Option | Five users, per year |
| --- | --- |
| Zoho CRM Standard | $840 |
| HubSpot Smart CRM Starter at list price | $1,200 |
| Zoho CRM Professional | $1,380 |
| Zoho CRM Enterprise | $2,400 |
| HubSpot Smart CRM Professional at $45 (blog annual figure) | $2,700 |

HubSpot's promotional Starter price of $7 would bring five seats to about $420 for the first annual term, but HubSpot describes it as a limited-time offer for new customers, so plan on the list price after that. At the $50 listed on HubSpot's Smart CRM page, Professional would be $3,000 for five users. HubSpot does not state whether the $7 promotion applies to every seat or only the first term, so check before you count on it. Taxes and add-ons are extra on both sides.

## Sales Hub is a different price

HubSpot also sells Sales Hub, its sales-specific product. Its Starter lists at $20 per seat per month, Professional at $100 and Enterprise at $150, and the pricing page we read shows Professional at $90 in a promotional or annual form. HubSpot's Sales Hub pricing page also lists one-time onboarding fees that are not included in the per-seat price, $3,500 for Enterprise and $1,500 for Professional, and says Starter uses Core Seats while Professional and Enterprise use Sales Seats. Zoho's own HubSpot comparison page sets Zoho CRM against Sales Hub, so its savings claims reflect Sales Hub prices. That page is written by Zoho, and its price tables are partly garbled in the copy we read, so read it as a vendor claim. HubSpot's own blog comparisons are likewise written by HubSpot.

The difference matters because a team that only needs shared contacts and a deal pipeline may be well served by Smart CRM, while a team that wants sequences, calling and forecasting is looking at Sales Hub prices.

## What each step up adds

From Zoho's published summaries, Standard adds sales forecasting, custom modules and email features on top of Free. Professional adds process management (Blueprints) and inventory management according to Zoho's current pages, with Zoho's older 2022 edition PDF also placing validation rules and web-to-case forms there, and Enterprise adds account-based marketing, a developer sandbox, custom buttons and functions, extended AI and field-level encryption.

HubSpot's Starter page says Starter removes HubSpot branding, allows unlimited paid seats, raises custom properties to 1,000 and adds email and in-app chat support. Its Smart CRM page lists duplicate merging and team organisation at Professional and custom objects and single sign-on at Enterprise.

The two vendors group features differently, so compare by the specific feature you need, not by edition names.

## Hidden costs to check

- **Storage and support:** Zoho's pricing pages mention paid support plans, and its 2022 edition PDF lists extra file storage as an add-on, so the editions may not include everything you need.
- **HubSpot Credits:** HubSpot's pages describe monthly credits for AI features, with extra credit packs sold separately.
- **Region:** Zoho's pages show rupee prices for India, and both vendors' taxes are extra.
- **Onboarding:** HubSpot's Sales Hub page lists one-time onboarding fees on top of the per-seat price.

## How to choose

- **Smallest budget for a few users:** Zoho Standard at $14 is cheaper than HubSpot Starter's $20 list price, but HubSpot's $7 and $10 new-customer promotions are lower while they last.
- **Staying free as long as possible:** compare the two free tiers first, since Zoho allows three users and HubSpot two.
- **One shared system across marketing, sales and support:** HubSpot's pitch is a single database across its products, which can justify a higher seat price if you will use several of them.
- **Process management on a budget:** Zoho Professional's Blueprints at $23 is the lowest-priced published step to process management. Basic workflow rules are already in Zoho's free edition.

Before you commit, ask both vendors for a written quote that includes seats, add-ons and renewal pricing, since promotions and list prices change.
